  "body": "Found by the read-only review of PR #2918 (comment of 2026-11-20). The finding comes from a read of the code. No run proves it yet.\n\n## What can happen\n\nA STEP file can use the short entity names of ISO 10303-21. The solidcore p21 parser changes some short names to the long names (`SHORT_NAMES`, `solidcore-record/src/step/p21.rs:614-659` at the pinned revision), for example `A2PL3D`, `CRTPNT`, `DRCTN` and `LNGUNT`. It does not change the short forms of `GLOBAL_UNIT_ASSIGNED_CONTEXT` and `SI_UNIT`.\n\nThe unit read of the model import (`length_units` in `camcraft-kernel`, and the units module of solidcore) finds only the long name `GLOBAL_UNIT_ASSIGNED_CONTEXT`. Thus a short-name file gives geometry but no unit context. If the reader then uses 1 mm for each unit, a model in inches or metres has the wrong size by a factor of 25.4 or 1000. No refusal or note tells the user.\n\nWrong geometry is wrong output for each toolpath and each gouge check. Thus the priority is `P1` until a test shows a refusal.\n\n## Where\n\n- `crates/kernel/src/solidcore_step.rs` (`length_units`, near line 244).\n- The same defect class in the tool-body read is a blocker of PR #2918 (`crates/geometry/src/step_csys_read.rs:28-30`, `crates/geometry/src/step_units.rs:37-62`). That PR repairs the tool-body read only.\n\n## Not known\n\n- If the reader before PR #2904 (truck-stepio) had the same result for short-name files.\n- If a CAD system that our users have writes short names. The form is legal but not common.\n\n## What is expected\n\n- A file that has geometry and a length-unit record, but no unit context that the reader can find, is refused by name. The unit laws of #306 and #689 apply.\n- Better: the reader knows the short names of the unit entities and reads the unit.\n\n## Done when\n\n- A STEP fixture that we made, with short entity names and an inch unit, is in `testdata/models/`.\n- A test imports it. The result is the correct size in millimetres, or a refusal that names the missing unit context. The result is never a model at 1 mm for each unit.\n- The same test set has a metre file.\n\nRefs #917\n",
